  "executive_briefing": "The 2026 corporate and international tax policy landscape is defined by the operationalization of OECD Pillar Two global minimum tax standards, driving extensive domestic tax code overhauls across approximately 140 jurisdictions. Sovereign governments are actively calibrating statutory corporate income tax rates and incentive regimes, particularly within regional blocs like ASEAN, to maintain economic competitiveness while preventing base erosion. Simultaneously, bilateral tariff actions monitored by global databases and national bodies add layers of trade policy volatility. On the compliance front, tax administrations are accelerating digital oversight, exemplified by Singapore setting firm 2030 and 2031 deadlines for mandatory e-invoicing built on the Peppol framework to enforce real-time continuous transaction controls.",
